    {
      "id": "sb-f-033",
      "len": 130,
      "source": "T:$\\displaystyle\\sum \\left(\\frac{1}{2^{n}} + \\frac{1}{3^{n}}\\right) = \\sum \\frac{1}{2^{n}} + \\sum \\frac{1}{3^{n}}$ (both converge)"
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-f-008",
      "len": 128,
      "source": "T:Solve $f(x) = g(x)$; the smallest and largest solutions are the limits, and one integral always covers everything between them"
    },
    {
      "id": "ar-f-008",
      "len": 126,
      "source": "T:Solve $f(x) = g(x)$; the smallest and largest solutions are the limits, and solutions between them are possible split points"
    },
    {
      "id": "sb-f-010",
      "len": 120,
      "source": "T:$\\lim a_n \\ne 0$ (or DNE) $\\Rightarrow \\sum a_n$ diverges, for geometric series only; $\\lim a_n = 0$ tells you nothing"
    },
    {
      "id": "st-f-009",
      "len": 119,
      "source": "T:$f(x) = \\frac{\\sin^2 x}{x^2}$ is not decreasing; limit comparison with $\\frac{1}{n^2}$ gives $L = 1$, so it converges"
    },
    {
      "id": "st-f-009",
      "len": 119,
      "source": "T:$f(x) = \\frac{\\sin^2 x}{x^2}$ is not continuous; limit comparison with $\\frac{1}{n^2}$ gives $L = 1$, so it converges"
    },
    {
      "id": "st-s-05#1",
